# PART 12. MODIFICATION SCENARIOS

## 12.1 Purpose

This section demonstrates how the database can be modified when requirements change.

The following scenarios show how new information can be inserted, updated, or removed without redesigning the entire database.

---

## 12.2 Scenario 1 — Add a New Participant

A new participant joins the competition.

```sql
INSERT INTO participant
(school_id, first_name, last_name, age, email)
VALUES
(1, 'Aruzhan', 'Bekova', 17, 'aruzhan.bekova@example.com');
```

The participant is now stored in the database and can later be assigned to a team.

---

## 12.3 Scenario 2 — Add a Participant to a Team

The new participant joins an existing team.

```sql
INSERT INTO team_participant
(team_id, participant_id, role)
VALUES
(1, 21, 'Programmer');
```

This creates a relationship between the participant and the team.

---

## 12.4 Scenario 3 — Update a Team Coach

Suppose the coach of a team changes.

```sql
UPDATE team
SET coach_name = 'Aidos Saparov'
WHERE team_id = 1;
```

Only the required record is modified.

---

## 12.5 Scenario 4 — Update a Robot Status

Suppose a robot is repaired and becomes active.

```sql
UPDATE robot
SET status = 'Active'
WHERE robot_id = 1;
```

This changes the robot's current status.

---

## 12.6 Scenario 5 — Register a Team for a New Competition

A team can be registered for another competition.

```sql
INSERT INTO competition_team
(competition_id, team_id, registration_date)
VALUES
(5, 1, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);
```

The existing team can therefore participate in another competition without creating a duplicate team record.

---

## 12.7 Additional Modification — Update a Score

If an authorized correction is required, a score can be updated.

```sql
UPDATE score
SET technical_score = 37.00,
    creativity_score = 27.00,
    performance_score = 28.00
WHERE score_id = 1;
```

The generated `total_score` is automatically recalculated by PostgreSQL.

---

## 12.8 Modification Principles

The database structure allows changes without unnecessary duplication.

For example:

* a participant can change teams;
* a team can enter another competition;
* a coach can be changed;
* robot status can be updated;
* competition scores can be corrected;
* new competitions can be added.

## 12.9 Conclusion

The normalized database structure makes it possible to modify individual records while preserving relationships and data integrity. Primary keys, foreign keys, unique constraints, and check constraints continue to protect the database during modifications.
